;; TEST-EXPECT: FAIL
;; CHECK-FAIL: "Crisp has no LET"
;;
;; `let*` is not a Crisp form and must be rejected.
;;
;; Crisp's own LET is ALREADY SEQUENTIAL -- later bindings see earlier ones, and it
;; destructures multiple values (see 01-let-unified, and 167/09 which documents the
;; delegation).  So `let*` is not merely unsupported, it is redundant: there is nothing it
;; could mean that `let` does not already do.
;;
;; WHY THIS NEEDS A GUARD RATHER THAN A NOTE.  `:crisp-language` deliberately does not
;; import let/let* from CL (see the shadowing-import block in src/package.lisp), so `let*`
;; read from Crisp source was MINTED as a fresh symbol in that package -- the same trap that
;; mints MOD.  It was then registered as an alias of analyze-let-expression, so it compiled
;; forward and produced byte-identical SPIR-V.
;;
;; But only the expression analyzer knew.  The uniformity pre-pass matches the operator name
;; "LET" exactly, and the AD walk runs before semantic analysis and knows only `let`.  So a
;; kernel using `let*` compiled and ran correctly until someone added --differentiate, at
;; which point it failed with
;;
;;     Function SET! is not differentiable.
;;
;; naming an operator that is perfectly differentiable and sending the reader into the
;; backward pass.  Found in endeavour 173, where all four A|D specs failed this way with no
;; shuffle involved.
;;
;; A form that works forward, is wrong backward, and blames a bystander is worse than one
;; that is simply refused.  So it is refused.

(def-function let-star-binding (a)
  (declare #'(int => int))
  (let* ((x (+ a 1))
         (y (* x 2)))
    (return y)))
